# Every text file checks out with LF, on every platform.
#
# This is not a style preference - it is what makes the byte-stability ratchet mean
# anything. A member resolved from the local search path is read as BYTES and its length
# goes into the retrieval report (`bytes: 128`). Left to git's autocrlf, a Windows checkout
# gets CRLF and every one of those counts is two bytes per line larger than the same
# checkout on Linux - so `tools/byteproof.py --check` fails on a clone that changed
# nothing, and the reports this tool emits would differ by where it was cloned.
#
# Mainframe members are fixed-format source besides, where a byte count is a fact about the
# member rather than an artifact of the filesystem it landed on.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the files whose exact bytes are the contract.
*.py      text eol=lf
*.asm     text eol=lf
*.mac     text eol=lf
*.cpy     text eol=lf
*.jcl     text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.toml    text eol=lf
*.sha256  text eol=lf
